Jing's canonical CBOR value codec and portable numeric operations\n     3\t   (docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md, phases J1 and J2: JVM, ClojureScript\n     4\t   and Dart).\n     5\t\n     6\t   `encode` turns one supported value into its canonical payload bytes;\n     7\t   `decode` accepts exactly one canonical payload and returns its value.\n     8\t   The byte contract is the frozen corpus test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\n     9\t   and the rulings in its README; this namespace implements it, it does not\n    10\t   define it.\n    11\t\n    12\t   Encoding normalizes a value into a wire tree (see `dao.jing.cbor.boring`)\n    13\t   and lets Boring write the bytes:\n    14\t\n    15\t   * keywords and symbols: tag 27 `dao.jing/keyword` / `dao.jing/symbol`\n    16\t     over `[namespace name]`, read from the identifier's fields;\n    17\t   * lists and finite seqs: tag 27 `dao.jing/list` over the item array;\n    18\t     vectors are plain arrays;\n    19\t   * floating point: tag 27 `dao.jing/float64` over eight big-endian bytes,\n    20\t     float32 widened exactly, every NaN made 7ff8000000000000;\n    21\t   * integers: CBOR integers, tags 2/3 beyond 64 bits;\n    22\t   * decimals: tag 4 `[exponent mantissa]`; ratios: tag 30, reduced;\n    23\t   * sets: tag 258 over elements sorted by their canonical bytes;\n    24\t   * metadata on collections and symbols: tag 27 `clojure/with-meta` over\n    25\t     `[meta value]`, outermost, after stripping unqualified :line :column\n    26\t     :end-line :end-column (validated before stripping) and omitting empty\n    27\t     metadata.\n    28\t\n    29\t   Decoding reads the bytes with Jing's own structural reader, which checks\n    30\t   every tag, frame name and payload shape before any host conversion (the\n    31\t   plan's \\\"validate before any lossy host conversion\\\"), then requires the\n    32\t   decoded value to re-encode to the identical bytes. Boring's decoder is not\n    33\t   used: its native mappings (tag-30 ratios collapsing denominator 1,\n    34\t   stringref and date tags, with-meta, a dropped trailing index frame, and\n    35\t   JavaScript numbers that cannot tell 1.0 from 1) would decide Jing bytes.\n    36\t\n    37\t   Every refusal is an ex-info whose data carries `::refusal`, one of the\n    38\t   corpus refusal classes as a keyword (`:non-canonical`,\n    39\t   `:unpaired-surrogate`, ...); `refusal` reads it.\n    40\t\n    41\t   Numbers: the JVM keeps its native exact kinds (long, BigInt, Double,\n    42\t   BigDecimal, Ratio) and adds a `Rational` carrier for denominator-1\n    43\t   ratios. ClojureScript uses safe-integer numbers and BigInt for integers,\n    44\t   and carriers for float64, decimal and rational content. Host `=` cannot\n    45\t   compare carriers with native numbers symmetrically, so the portable\n    46\t   operations are explicit functions: `num=`, `num-hash`, `num-compare`, and\n    47\t   the recursive `equiv` / `equiv-hash`.\n    48\t\n    49\t   Dart (J2): the same reader, profile and portable operations run on\n    50\t   ClojureDart. The byte writer there is the pinned `cbor` 6.5.1 package\n    51\t   behind `dao.jing.cbor.cljd`, which implements the same wire vocabulary as\n    52\t   `dao.jing.cbor.boring`. Dart integers are 64-bit ints and BigInt, floats\n    53\t   are native doubles (a distinct type from int on the Dart VM), and decimals\n    54\t   and ratios use the Decimal and Rational carriers. Every host reader\n    55\t   conditional names `:cljd` first.\"\n    56\t  (:refer-clojure :exclude [decimal? ratio?])\n    57\t  (:require\n    58\t    #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:convert\" :as convert]\n    59\t               [dao.jing.cbor.cljd :as wire]]\n    60\t        :default [[dao.jing.cbor.boring :as wire]]))\n    61\t  #?@(:cljd [(:import\n    62\t               [\"dart:core\" BigInt FormatException String]\n    63\t               [\"dart:typed_data\" ByteData Uint8List])]\n    64\t      :clj [(:import\n    65\t              (java.math\n    66\t                BigDecimal\n    67\t                BigInteger)\n    68\t              (java.nio\n    69\t                ByteBuffer)\n    70\t              (java.nio.charset\n    71\t                CharacterCodingException\n    72\t                CodingErrorAction\n    73\t                StandardCharsets))]))\n    74\t\n    75\t\n    76\t;; ==========================================================================\n    77\t;; Refusals\n    78\t;; ==========================================================================\n    79\t\n    80\t(defn- refuse\n    81\t  \"Throw the refusal `cls` (a corpus refusal class keyword).\"\n    82\t  ([cls] (refuse cls nil))\n    83\t  ([cls detail]\n    84\t   (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.jing.cbor refused: \" (name cls)\n    85\t                        (when detail (str \" (\" detail \")\")))\n    86\t                   {::refusal cls :detail detail}))))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(defn- type-name\n    90\t  \"The host type of x, for refusal details.\"\n    91\t  [x]\n    92\t  #?(:cljd (str (.-runtimeType x))\n    93\t     :default (str (type x))))\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t(defn refusal\n    97\t  \"The refusal class keyword of a thrown codec refusal, or nil.\"\n    98\t  [ex]\n    99\t  (::refusal (ex-data ex)))\n   100\t\n   101\t\n   102\t;; ==========================================================================\n   103\t;; Exact host big integers: BigInteger on the JVM, BigInt on JavaScript\n   104\t;; ==========================================================================\n   105\t\n   106\t#?(:cljs\n   107\t   (defn- bigint?\n   108\t     [x]\n   109\t     (identical? \"bigint\" (js* \"typeof ~{}\" x))))\n   110\t\n   111\t\n   112\t(defn- big\n   113\t  \"An exact host big integer from a host integer or a decimal string.\n   114\t   Anything else (a float, a non-integral number) is refused loudly\n   115\t   rather than truncated.\"\n   116\t  [x]\n   117\t  #?(:cljd (cond (dart/is? x BigInt) x\n   118\t                 (dart/is? x int) (BigInt.from x)\n   119\t                 (string? x) (BigInt.parse x)\n   120\t                 :else (refuse :unsupported-value\n   121\t                               (str \"not an exact integer: \" x)))\n   122\t     :clj (cond (instance? BigInteger x) x\n   123\t                (instance? clojure.lang.BigInt x)\n   124\t                (.toBigInteger ^clojure.lang.BigInt x)\n   125\t                (string? x) (BigInteger. ^String x)\n   126\t                (or (instance? Long x) (instance? Integer x)\n   127\t                    (instance? Short x) (instance? Byte x))\n   128\t                (BigInteger/valueOf (long x))\n   129\t                :else (refuse :unsupported-value\n   130\t                              (str \"not an exact integer: \" (type x))))\n   131\t     :cljs (if (and (number? x) (not (js/Number.isInteger x)))\n   132\t             (refuse :unsupported-value (str \"not an exact integer: \" x))\n   133\t             (js/BigInt x))))\n   134\t\n   135\t\n   136\t(def ^:private zero (big 0))\n   137\t(def ^:private one (big 1))\n   138\t(def ^:private two (big 2))\n   139\t(def ^:private ten (big 10))\n   140\t\n   141\t\n   142\t(defn- b+\n   143\t  [a b]\n   144\t  #?(:cljd (. ^BigInt a \"+\" b)\n   145\t     :clj (.add ^BigInteger a ^BigInteger b)\n   146\t     :cljs (js* \"(~{} + ~{})\" a b)))\n   147\t\n   148\t\n   149\t(defn- b-\n   150\t  [a b]\n   151\t  #?(:cljd (. ^BigInt a \"-\" b)\n   152\t     :clj (.subtract ^BigInteger a ^BigInteger b)\n   153\t     :cljs (js* \"(~{} - ~{})\" a b)))\n   154\t\n   155\t\n   156\t(defn- b*\n   157\t  [a b]\n   158\t  #?(:cljd (. ^BigInt a \"*\" b)\n   159\t     :clj (.multiply ^BigInteger a ^BigInteger b)\n   160\t     :cljs (js* \"(~{} * ~{})\" a b)))\n   161\t\n   162\t\n   163\t(defn- bquot\n   164\t  \"Quotient truncated toward zero.\"\n   165\t  [a b]\n   166\t  #?(:cljd (. ^BigInt a \"~/\" b)\n   167\t     :clj (.divide ^