{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '135,190p;400,510p'\"","aggregated_output":"   135\tbecome part of an index payload's identity. The same index payload published\n   136\tthrough a different intake stream receives the same address.\n   137\t\n   138\tPublication acknowledges that every payload was appended to the selected\n   139\tintake stream. It does not acknowledge that an asynchronous observer has yet\n   140\tmaterialized those payloads; see *Open items and current limitations*.\n   141\t\n   142\tPublication changes representation and access cost, not the meaning of the\n   143\tcovered data. Consumers whose explicit read coordinates name the published\n   144\tmanifest address can consume the persisted covered indexes.\n   145\t\n   146\t## Materialization rule\n   147\t\n   148\tThe materialization is an idempotent union of content-addressed values. If\n   149\t`H(x)` is the address of the canonical encoding of `x`, observing `x` means:\n   150\t\n   151\t```clojure\n   152\t(assoc-if-absent target (H x) x)\n   153\t```\n   154\t\n   155\tRe-observing the same value is a no-op. Observing equal values through\n   156\tdifferent pool streams is also a no-op after the first insertion. Consequently:\n   157\t\n   158\t- replay is safe;\n   159\t- pool streams may be consumed in any interleaving;\n   160\t- duplicate publication is harmless;\n   161\t- immutable content writes do not require cross-stream coordination; and\n   162\t- a materialization can be reconstructed by replaying the available pool\n   163\t  streams.\n   164\t\n   165\tThe implemented write is `dao.jing/materialize!`: it derives the address from\n   166\tthe payload alone (`segment-key`) and asks the backend's `:put-content-fn`\n   167\tfor an explicit verdict. A backend validates before it writes: the address must\n   168\tbe a segment address and must hash to the payload, else it throws and stores\n   169\tnothing. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\n   170\t`:present` means an equal value is already stored there, in which case the\n   171\tstored value is read back and verified. A collision in which an existing\n   172\taddress contains different canonical bytes is an integrity failure, not an\n   173\toverwrite, and throws loudly. `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence:\n   174\t`get` takes a caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n   175\t\n   176\t### Canonical encoding\n   177\t\n   178\tContent addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\n   179\tsame bytes on every participating platform. Distinct values must address\n   180\tdistinctly, both across types (`42` and `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one\n   181\ttype (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not). Minted addresses are readable EDN: the\n   182\tname never starts with a digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`.\n   183\tThe canonical encoder is therefore part of the storage contract even though the\n   184\tmeaning of the encoded value is not.\n   185\t\n   186\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   187\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   188\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   189\t\n   190\tThe target encoding is a canonical flat byte representation suitable for\n   400\tImplemented backends:\n   401\t\n   402\t- `dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem` — an ephemeral, thread-safe,\n   403\t  content-addressed in-memory store. Put is an atomic insert-if-absent; an\n   404\t  address already holding the same payload reports `:present` and is never\n   405\t  overwritten.\n   406\t- `dao.jing.file/create-content-file` — a content-addressed store backed by a\n   407\t  private framed append-only file. Each log record is `[address payload]`, written\n   408\t  through a write lock, acknowledged only after the log is flushed, and\n   409\t  replayed on open to rebuild the in-memory content map. The framing layer\n   410\t  truncates an incomplete tail before replay; Jing then fails closed on any\n   411\t  complete record that cannot be decoded, validated, or matched to its content\n   412\t  address. The store guarantees idempotent close, throws after close, and\n   413\t  serializes concurrent puts with exactly one record written.\n   414\t- `dao.jing.remote` — over DaoStream v2, both halves JVM-only: the\n   415\t  constructor `connect-content!` returns a content handle over a live v2\n   416\t  attachment, and `serve-content!` serves `default-handlers` — or any\n   417\t  `{op fn}` map — at a WebSocket endpoint. The synchronous client is a\n   418\t  blocking driver as host policy over the portable non-waiting call step:\n   419\t  a JVM thread polls and sleeps between advances, and the cadence\n   420\t  (`:poll-interval-ms`), the connect deadline (`:connect-timeout-ms`) and\n   421\t  the request deadline (`:request-timeout-ms`) are options of the\n   422\t  constructor, not constants of the step. The constructor returns only\n   423\t  after the attachment reports `/established`; a failed open — a terminal\n   424\t  lifecycle, the deadline, or an interruption of the establishment loop's\n   425\t  sleep — guarantees only that the handle is closed and nothing escapes to\n   426\t  the caller — on\n   427\t  the JVM a close before the socket opens does not tear down the JDK's\n   428\t  establishment, so a peer that accepts TCP and never completes the\n   429\t  upgrade costs one held connection per attempt for the process lifetime\n   430\t  (the establishment-cancel gap `dao.stream.ws.md` records). The traffic\n   431\t  cursor is minted at `:dao.stream/newest` **before** `attach!`, because\n   432\t  the host may deposit the establishment event at any moment after\n   433\t  attaching and a cursor minted later would sit past it, hanging every\n   434\t  connect to its timeout. A URL is `ws://host[:port][/path]`, port\n   435\t  defaulting to 80 and an absent path naming `/jing`; `wss://`, a missing\n   436\t  host, a port that is not a positive integer, and a bracketed IPv6\n   437\t  authority throw before any socket. The wire vocabulary is\n   438\t  `:jing/put-content` answering `:inserted`/`:present` and\n   439\t  `:jing/get-content` answering exactly `{:found? boolean :value v}`, so\n   440\t  a stored `nil` is distinguishable from absence. One client carries one\n   441\t  locally awaited call — calls serialize under the client's lock — while\n   442\t  `close!` runs outside that lock and is safe during an in-flight call,\n   443\t  which then throws the terminal reason. A timed-out call retires its\n   444\t  bookkeeping (the id leaves the outstanding table and its late response\n   445\t  is dropped as unsolicited) but not the remote execution, which the\n   446\t  server may still be running. Bookkeeping is bounded by the one call in\n   447\t  flight: every exit of the blocking driver — return, timeout, terminal,\n   448\t  an immediate refusal at the writer, or an interruption of its poll sleep\n   449\t  — drains its completions and diagnostics before storing state, because an\n   450\t  exit that stored nothing would let the next call reuse the interrupted\n   451\t  call's request id and take its late response as an answer. An interrupted\n   452\t  call retires the same way a timed-out one does, and the thread's interrupt\n   453\t  flag is re-asserted before the throw. The timing options themselves are\n   454\t  validated at the driver's entry, before anything is sent: an argument\n   455\t  defect must throw before the wire, or it strands a request whose id the\n   456\t  next call would reuse. The portable value domain binds in\n   457\t  both directions: a payload outside it is refused before anything is\n   458\t  sent, and a handler result outside it is answered as a correlated\n   459\t  `:dao.jing.remote/non-portable-result` error rather than a timeout.\n   460\t  After a terminal lifecycle every call throws with that reason and the\n   461\t  handle is never rebound; reattachment is the caller's, by opening a new\n   462\t  coordinate. Diagnostics have no outlet under a blocking driver and are\n   463\t  drained and dropped at every step. Server `stop!` detaches every\n   464\t  session, releases the listener, and is idempotent; handlers run in the\n   465\t  server's single driver thread, so a handler that never returns stalls\n   466\t  every session.\n   467\t- `dao.jing.dht/create-content-dht` and\n   468\t  `dao.jing.dht.node/create-content-dht-udp` — the distributed backend over an\n   469\t  `IDhtNet` transport; see `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md`.\n   470\t\n   471\t**The observer is implemented.** `observer-state` and `observe-step!` provide\n   472\tthe explicit intake-pool walk described in *Cursor tracking and recovery*,\n   473\twith no atoms, globals, registration, or discovery. The source stream never\n   474\tenters an address or a stored value.\n   475\t\n   476\t**Content addressing is implemented via the closed multihash registry.**\n   477\t`content-hash` digests canonical bytes under the selected algorithm (:blake3\n   478\tdefault, :sha256 selectable); `segment-key` mints\n   479\t`:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>` addresses; `segment-address?` is the strict\n   480\taddress test the backend layer enforces; `segment-matches?` is the total\n   481\taddress-directed verification predicate. As recorded in *Canonical encoding*,\n   482\tthe encoder is transitional until the pinned canonical CBOR encoding lands.\n   483\t\n   484\t## Open items and current limitations\n   485\t\n   486\t- **Canonical encoding.** The order-normalized hand-printer encoder must be\n   487\t  replaced by a pinned, cross-platform canonical byte encoding. Until then,\n   488\t  content addresses are portable only between implementations sharing the\n   489\t  exact print rule; when the encoding lands, `content-hash`, `segment-key`,\n   490\t  and every minted address change together. Three residuals of the\n   491\t  transitional encoder are deferred to that landing: scalar (symbol)\n   492\t  metadata is not address-significant; pathological symbols whose print text\n   493\t  mimics another value's print (e.g. `(symbol \"42\")` vs `42`) can collide;\n   494\t  and ambient print-var bindings (`*print-readably*` and similar) still\n   495\t  reach scalar bytes, since `canonical-print` delegates scalars to `pr-str`\n   496\t  — collection structure and order are rendered by `canonical-print`\n   497\t  itself, so only scalar leaves reach the host printer.\n   498\t- **ClojureDart's `list` mints metadata.** On ClojureDart, `(list ...)` and\n   499\t  `(apply list ...)` return a list carrying `cljd.core`'s own reader metadata\n   500\t  (`{:line … :column … :end-line … :end-column … :tag PersistentList}`).\n   501\t  `order-normalize`, `yin.vm`'s semantic-bytecode projection, and the\n   502\t  ClojureDart Transit decoders (`dao.stream.transit.cljd` behind\n   503\t  `dao.stream.ws`'s incoming frames, and the older `dao.stream.transit`)\n   504\t  clear metadata on the lists they mint, so neither normalization nor a list\n   505\t  decoded off the wire fabricates it. Any other Dart code that builds a\n   506\t  payload with `list` still hands `dao.jing` that metadata, and its `:tag`\n   507\t  survives the reader-position strip, so the payload addresses differently\n   508\t  from the equal list built on another host. Each such constructor must clear\n   509\t  it the same way (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`) until the ClojureDart\n   510\t  defect is fixed upstream or the pinned canonical encoding decides the fate\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
