{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '190,245p'\"","aggregated_output":"   190\tThe target encoding is a canonical flat byte representation suitable for\n   191\tcross-platform hashing and in-place reading. The current implementation uses\n   192\tan order-normalized, metadata-aware hand printer (`dao.jing/order-normalize`\n   193\tand `canonical-print`) as a transitional encoder — deterministic and\n   194\torder-insensitive, but not yet the pinned canonical byte encoding. This is the\n   195\tfirst open item under *Open items and current limitations*.\n   196\t\n   197\tThe transitional encoder's current contract, precisely: collection metadata\n   198\t(on maps, sets, vectors, lists, and seqs) is address-significant, except\n   199\treader-position keys (`:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, `:end-column`), which\n   200\tare stripped before hashing, and empty metadata, which is dropped rather than\n   201\ttreated as distinct from no metadata. Scalar metadata (on symbols — no\n   202\tportable host lets a keyword carry metadata) is not address-significant\n   203\t— it is silently ignored, a known\n   204\tresidual pending the pinned canonical byte encoding. Lists and seqs of equal\n   205\tcontent share one address (`=` calls them equal and both print the same way);\n   206\tvectors, sets, and maps are each their own type and never collide with\n   207\tanother, for any non-pathological scalar (see the pathological-symbol\n   208\tresidual under *Open items and current limitations*). Records are not a\n   209\tsupported payload: `content-hash` throws rather\n   210\tthan silently addressing a record as its equal plain map, since the\n   211\tparticipating hosts cannot agree on how to print one. `materialize!`'s\n   212\t`:present` read-back is verified by `segment-matches?`, ensuring that\n   213\ta metadata-only mismatch is caught as a real collision.\n   214\t\n   215\t## Multihash Content Addressing & Algorithm Registry\n   216\t\n   217\tDaoJing implements a closed, immutable algorithm registry providing\n   218\tmulti-algorithm content-addressing across JVM, Node/CLJS, and ClojureDart.\n   219\t\n   220\tSix core concepts are strictly distinguished:\n   221\t\n   222\t1. **Algorithm:** A registered cryptographic hash function identified by a\n   223\t   keyword (`:blake3` or `:sha256`). The registry is closed and immutable:\n   224\t   - `:blake3`: default minting algorithm; produces 32-byte (256-bit) digests.\n   225\t   - `:sha256`: permanent first-class selectable algorithm; produces 32-byte\n   226\t     digests.\n   227\t2. **Digest:** The lowercase hexadecimal representation of the raw digest\n   228\t   bytes computed over `canonical-bytes(payload)`.\n   229\t3. **Address:** The namespace-qualified keyword\n   230\t   `:segment/<algorithm-id>-<digest-hex>` (81-character keyword\n   231\t   beginning with `:segment/blake3-` or `:segment/sha256-`).\n   232\t   Validated by `dao.jing/segment-address?` and parsed by\n   233\t   `dao.jing/parse-segment-address`.\n   234\t4. **Minting:** Deriving a new address for content (`dao.jing/content-hash`,\n   235\t   `dao.jing/segment-key`, `dao.jing/materialize!`). Implicit minting defaults\n   236\t   to `:blake3`. Explicit minting supports `{:algorithm :sha256}` across public\n   237\t   APIs.\n   238\t5. **Verification:** Validating that an opaque payload matches an existing\n   239\t   address using `dao.jing/segment-matches?`.\n   240\t   - **Verification must be address-directed; minting primitives are not\n   241\t     validators.**\n   242\t   - The address carries its own algorithm identifier. `segment-matches?` parses\n   243\t     the address, computes canonical bytes, digests them using the algorithm\n   244\t     indicated by the address, and compares. It never consults\n   245\t     `default-hash-algorithm` and never throws on encoder refusal or\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
